{
  "_description": "Mock AI output for Tracer. One circuit block. 'schema' documents the contract; 'block' is the payload generator.py consumes.",
  "schema": {
    "block": {
      "name": "string  - human name of the circuit block",
      "sheet": "string - KiCad paper size, e.g. 'A4'. Controller is centred on this sheet.",
      "grid_mm": "number - snap grid. 1.27 = 50 mil, the KiCad schematic standard.",
      "layout": {
        "channel_mm": "number - clear routing channel reserved between adjacent columns",
        "row_pitch_mm": "number - optional override; default = tallest symbol + channel, snapped to grid",
        "body_margin_mm": "number - keep-out padding added around each symbol's pin extents"
      },
      "components": [
        {
          "ref": "string       - reference designator, e.g. 'R1'",
          "value": "string     - component value, e.g. '330'",
          "symbol": "string    - KiCad symbol lib id, e.g. 'Device:R'",
          "footprint": "string - KiCad footprint lib id",
          "column": "integer   - signal-flow stage. Sorted ascending = strict left-to-right placement.",
          "row": "integer      - vertical slot within the column. 0 = the controller's row (sheet centre); negative = above.",
          "rotation": "number  - optional, 0/90/180/270. Used to aim pins along the signal flow.",
          "role": "string      - optional. Exactly one component should be 'controller'; it is pinned dead centre."
        }
      ],
      "nets": [
        {
          "name": "string  - net name, e.g. '+3V3'",
          "class": "string - 'signal' (default) draws orthogonal wires; 'power' places net labels only",
          "connections": [
            { "ref": "string - component ref", "pin": "string - pin number" }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "_notes": [
      "x/y offsets from the previous schema are gone. Absolute coordinates are now derived by the layout",
      "engine from (column, row) so that placement is deterministic and always lands on the grid.",
      "Rails ('power' class) deliberately carry no wires - label-only rails are what stop an 8-pin GND",
      "net from becoming spaghetti across the whole sheet."
    ]
  },
